"Partitions a rectangle into nested areas proportional to additive values within a hierarchy.\n\nUse when: A hierarchical composition contains many positive leaf contributions. Compact overview and dominant branches matter more than exact value comparisons.\n\nAvoid when: Precise comparisons or numerous tiny labels are essential.\n\nVisual grammar: Leaf rectangle area encodes its nonnegative value. Containment encodes parent-child membership; fill may identify a branch or a separate declared measure.\n\nBuild: Validate a rooted hierarchy and aggregate leaf values upward. Allocate nested rectangles using a stated tiling rule. Reserve padding and parent headers, labeling only rectangles that can contain short text safely.\n\nCheck: Check child values sum to parent values without double-counting. Verify rectangle areas represent values after accounting for padding policy. Confirm small hidden labels remain available through an external key or detail list.\n\nPitfalls: Aspect ratios and changing layouts make similar areas hard to compare. Do not independently add parent values to descendants or place text across tile boundaries.\n\nExample: Show synthetic storage use by folder and file type with leaf area proportional to bytes and branch headers inside reserved padding.\n\nAlternatives: Icicle chart, Sunburst chart, Stacked bar chart."
